 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 59,
   "text": "SOUTH: Carlisle and England. NORTH: the Debatable Land, Gretna and the Scottish Borders. Cattle prices have not changed. The reivers' views on them have."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 55,
   "text": "For about three hundred years, from the end of the 13th century until the Union of the Crowns in 1603, families on both sides of the Anglo-Scottish border raided each other for cattle and goods. They included Armstrongs, Elliots, Grahams and Nixons, and many were loyal to their family before either crown. Our word 'blackmail' comes from the protection money farmers paid to be left alone.",
   "plaque": "w55e_border_reivers",
   "title": "The Border Reivers"
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 44,
   "text": "A strip of land between the rivers Esk and Sark that both England and Scotland claimed, and neither governed. It became a refuge for outlaws and reivers. In 1552 a border commission divided it with a ditch and bank, the Scotch Dyke, and the Debatable Land ceased to be debatable, at least on paper.",
   "plaque": "w55e_debatable_land",
   "title": "The Debatable Land"
  },
  {
   "x": 20,
   "y": 38,
   "text": "SARK. The river is the boundary here. The Sark was a name for trouble long before the toll bridge. Don't mind the dyke, it's mostly grass now."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "In 1754 Lord Hardwicke's Marriage Act tightened the rules for marrying in England, but Scotland's law stayed looser: young couples could marry there without a parent's consent. Gretna was the first village across the border on the main road north, and runaway couples hurried to the blacksmith's, who was as good as any priest. Thousands were married here, and people still are.",
   "plaque": "w55e_gretna",
   "title": "Gretna Green"
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "BORDER. A sign, a stone, a lay-by and a slightly different type of rain. You are in Scotland, or you will be in about eleven steps."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "A brooding fortress in Liddesdale, rebuilt in the 14th century and held by powerful border families. In October 1566 Mary, Queen of Scots, rode here from Jedburgh to visit her wounded favourite, the Earl of Bothwell, and back again, a round trip of about fifty miles. She fell dangerously ill soon after. The castle has a bad name for ghosts.",
   "plaque": "w55e_hermitage",
   "title": "Hermitage Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "NORTH: the Scottish Borders. Melrose, the Eildon Hills and a pig with a lot of opinions. SOUTH: Gretna, Longtown and Carlisle."
  }
 ],
